{"qid":"Q801","names":["State of Israel"],"title":"State of Israel","endonym":{"native":"دَوْلَة إِسْرَائِيل","lang":"ar"},"capsule":"The State of Israel declared independence on 14 May 1948 following the expiration of the British Mandate for Palestine, immediately fighting a war against five Arab states that ended in 1949 armistice lines. The 1967 Six-Day War extended Israeli control to the West Bank, Gaza Strip, Sinai Peninsula, and Golan Heights; Sinai was returned to Egypt under the 1979 Camp David Accords. Israel's modern economy, built around high-technology sectors and a compulsory defence system, made it the region's most industrialised state; it signed peace treaties with Egypt (1979) and Jordan (1994) and normalisation agreements with four Arab states under the 2020 Abraham Accords. The state's legal status and borders remain disputed, particularly regarding the West Bank and Gaza, which are the subject of ongoing international negotiations.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Israel's internationally recognised 1949 armistice lines are precisely documented; the extent of Israeli civil and military administration in the West Bank and Golan Heights is well-attested but remains contested under international law, making definitive boundary mapping politically complex.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Israel","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Israel"},{"label":"Britannica: Israel","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Israel"},{"label":"Wikipedia: 1948 Arab–Israeli War","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1948_Arab%E2%80%93Israeli_War"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Abraham Accords","url":